r\n* Uterus is held with appropriate traction.\r\n* Pelvic sidewalls are exposed.\r\n\r\nStep 3: Opening the broad ligament\r\n\r\n* Round ligament is identified, divided and ligated.\r\n* Broad ligament is opened.\r\n* The course of the ureter is identified and protected. \r\n\r\nStep 4: Pelvic lymphadenectomy\r\n\r\n* Pelvic lymph nodes are dissected when indicated.\r\n* Important structures such as the obturator nerve and vessels are identified and preserved. \r\n\r\nStep 5: Development of pelvic spaces\r\n\r\n* Paravesical space is developed.\r\n* Pararectal space is developed.\r\n* These spaces help identify important pelvic structures and facilitate safe dissection. \r\n\r\nStep 6: Uterine vessels\r\n\r\n* Uterine artery and associated vessels are identified.\r\n* Uterine vessels are ligated and divided near their origin as appropriate for the radical procedure. \r\n\r\nStep 7: Ureteric dissection\r\n\r\n* Ureter is carefully identified.\r\n* The ureter is dissected from surrounding tissues to allow safe removal of the parametrial tissues.\r\n* Care is taken to prevent ureteric injury.\r\n\r\nStep 8: Bladder mobilization\r\n\r\n* Bladder is separated and mobilized from the cervix and upper vagina.\r\n* Vesicouterine ligaments are carefully divided. \r\n\r\nStep 9: Posterior and lateral dissection\r\n\r\n* Uterosacral and rectovaginal/related supporting tissues are divided.\r\n* Parametrial and paracervical tissues are removed with the uterus and cervix.\r\n* Nerve-sparing techniques may be used where appropriate to reduce postoperative bladder, bowel and sexual dysfunction. \r\n\r\nStep 10: Removal of specimen\r\n\r\n* The uterus, cervix, parametrial tissues and upper vaginal cuff are removed as one specimen.\r\n* Surgical margins are assessed.\r\n\r\nStep 11: Vaginal closure\r\n\r\n* The vaginal cuff is closed.\r\n* Hemostasis is checked carefully.\r\n\r\nStep 12: Closure\r\n\r\n* Pelvic and abdominal structures are returned to appropriate positions.\r\n* Abdomen is closed in layers.\r\n* Specimens are sent for histopathological examination. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Flowchart of Surgical Steps\r\n\r\nAnesthesia + catheterization\r\n↓\r\nAbdominal incision and exploration\r\n↓\r\nOpen broad ligament and identify ureters\r\n↓\r\nPelvic lymphadenectomy\r\n↓\r\nDevelop paravesical and pararectal spaces\r\n↓\r\nLigate uterine vessels\r\n↓\r\nMobilize bladder and dissect ureters\r\n↓\r\nDivide parametrial, uterosacral and related ligaments\r\n↓\r\nRemove uterus + cervix + parametrium + upper vagina\r\n↓\r\nClose vaginal cuff\r\n↓\r\nHemostasis and abdominal closure\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Important Complications\r\n\r\n* Hemorrhage.\r\n* Bladder injury.\r\n* Ureteric injury.\r\n* Bowel injury.\r\n* Urinary dysfunction.\r\n* Lymphocele.\r\n* Lymphedema.\r\n* Infection.\r\n* Venous thromboembolism.\r\n* Sexual dysfunction.\r\n* Bladder and bowel dysfunction. /////////////////////////////                                                                                   Herniorrhaphy, Colostomy and Ileostomy\r\n\r\nIndications and Surgery step \r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. HERNIORRHAPHY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Herniorrhaphy is the surgical repair of a hernia by reducing the herniated contents and repairing the defect in the abdominal wall.\r\n* In modern practice, many hernia repairs use mesh reinforcement, depending on the type and characteristics of the hernia. \r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Symptomatic hernia causing pain or discomfort.\r\n* Enlarging hernia.\r\n* Irreducible/incarcerated hernia.\r\n* Strangulated hernia.\r\n* Intestinal obstruction due to hernia.\r\n* Recurrent hernia.\r\n* Risk of complications such as obstruction or strangulation.\r\n* Cosmetic or functional problems in selected patients. \r\n\r\nSurgical Steps of Open Herniorrhaphy\r\n\r\n1. Pre-operative preparation\r\n    * Patient assessment and consent.\r\n    * General, regional or local anesthesia depending on the procedure.\r\n    * Surgical site prepared and draped.\r\n2. Incision\r\n    * Incision is made over the hernia site.\r\n3. Exposure\r\n    * Skin and subcutaneous tissues are dissected.\r\n    * Hernia sac is identified.\r\n4. Dissection of hernia sac\r\n    * Sac is carefully separated from surrounding structures.\r\n    * In inguinal hernia, the sac is dissected from the spermatic cord structures when applicable. \r\n5. Reduction of contents\r\n    * Herniated bowel, omentum or other tissue is returned to the abdominal cavity.\r\n    * Non-viable tissue may require resection.\r\n6. Management of hernia sac\r\n    * Sac is ligated and/or excised as appropriate.\r\n7. Repair of abdominal-wall defect\r\n    * Fascial defect is repaired with sutures and/or reinforced with mesh depending on the operation. \r\n8. Closure\r\n    * Hemostasis is achieved.\r\n    * Wound is closed in layers.\r\n\r\nFlowchart\r\n\r\nIncision → Identify hernia → Dissect sac → Reduce contents → Manage sac → Repair/reinforce defect → Wound closure\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. COLOSTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Colostomy is a surgical procedure in which a portion of the colon is brought through an opening in the abdominal wall to form a stoma, allowing feces to pass into an external appliance. \r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Colorectal cancer.\r\n* Intestinal obstruction.\r\n* Bowel perforation.\r\n* Traumatic bowel injury.\r\n* Diverticular disease with obstruction or perforation.\r\n* Inflammatory bowel disease.\r\n* Protection of a distal colorectal/intestinal anastomosis.\r\n* Severe perineal or anorectal disease.\r\n* Fecal incontinence in selected cases.\r\n* Temporary diversion when the distal bowel requires healing.\r\n* Permanent diversion when the colon/rectum/anus cannot be restored to normal function. \r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n* End colostomy\r\n* Loop colostomy\r\n* Double-barrel colostomy\r\n* May be temporary or permanent.\r\n\r\nSurgical Steps of Colostomy\r\n\r\n1. Pre-operative preparation\r\n    * Patient assessment and consent.\r\n    * Stoma site is ideally marked with consideration of body position, skin folds, scars and bony prominences. \r\n2. Anesthesia\r\n    * Usually performed under general anesthesia.\r\n3. Abdominal access\r\n    * Open or laparoscopic approach may be used.\r\n4. Identify the colon\r\n    * Diseased or damaged segment is identified.\r\n    * Bowel may be resected if required.\r\n5. Select the stoma site\r\n    * An opening is created through the abdominal wall, commonly through the rectus muscle.\r\n6. Bring colon through abdominal wall\r\n    * The selected segment of colon is delivered through the stoma opening without tension or twisting.\r\n7. Create the stoma\r\n    * The colon is opened and its edges are sutured to the surrounding skin.\r\n    * The stoma is matured.\r\n8. Closure\r\n    * Abdominal incision is closed.\r\n    * Ostomy pouch is applied.\r\n\r\nFlowchart\r\n\r\nAnesthesia → Abdominal access → Identify colon → Resect/bypass diseased segment → Create abdominal opening → Bring colon out → Mature stoma → Close wound → Apply pouch\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. ILEOSTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Ileostomy is a surgical procedure in which the ileum is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma, allowing intestinal contents to leave the body through the stoma. \r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Ulcerative colitis.\r\n* Crohn’s disease involving the colon.\r\n* Colorectal cancer.\r\n* Total or subtotal colectomy.\r\n* Proctocolectomy.\r\n* Intestinal obstruction.\r\n* Severe bowel injury.\r\n* Bowel perforation.\r\n* Protection of a distal intestinal anastomosis.\r\n* Temporary fecal diversion.\r\n* Complicated colorectal surgery.\r\n\r\nIleostomy may be temporary or permanent, depending on the underlying disease and whether bowel continuity can later be restored. \r\n\r\nTypes\r\n\r\n* End ileostomy\r\n* Loop ileostomy\r\n* Continent ileostomy in selected cases.\r\n\r\nSurgical Steps of Ileostomy\r\n\r\n1. Pre-operative preparation\r\n    * Patient assessment and consent.\r\n    * Stoma site is selected and marked.\r\n2. Anesthesia\r\n    * Usually performed under general anesthesia.\r\n3. Abdominal access\r\n    * Open or laparoscopic approach may be used. \r\n4. Identify the ileum\r\n    * Terminal ileum is identified.\r\n    * Adequate blood supply and mobility are confirmed.\r\n5. Resection if required\r\n    * Diseased bowel may be removed according to the primary operation.\r\n6. Create stoma opening\r\n    * An opening is created through the abdominal wall.\r\n7. Bring ileum through the abdominal wall\r\n    * Ileal segment is brought out without twisting or excessive tension.\r\n8. Mature the ileostomy\r\n    * Ileum is opened and sutured to the skin.\r\n    * An ileostomy is generally everted to form a spout. \r\n9. Closure\r\n    * Abdominal wound is closed.\r\n    * Ileostomy appliance is fitted.\r\n\r\nFlowchart\r\n\r\nAnesthesia → Abdominal access → Identify ileum → Resect diseased bowel if required → Create stoma opening → Bring ileum out → Evert/mature stoma → Close wound → Apply pouch ///////////////////                   Cystectomy, Appendectomy and Cholecystectomy\r\n\r\nIndications and Surgical Steps\r\n\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. CYSTECTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Cystectomy is the surgical removal of the urinary bladder, either partially or completely.\r\n* Partial cystectomy: only part of the bladder is removed.\r\n* Radical cystectomy: the entire bladder is removed, usually with pelvic lymph-node dissection and urinary diversion. \r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Muscle-invasive bladder cancer.\r\n* High-risk or recurrent bladder cancer not adequately controlled by other treatment.\r\n* Extensive bladder tumour involving multiple areas.\r\n* Selected localized bladder tumours suitable for partial cystectomy.\r\n* Severe bladder disease in selected cases.\r\n* Palliative removal in selected patients with uncontrolled bleeding, pain or fistula. \r\n\r\nSurgical Steps of Radical Cystectomy\r\n\r\n1. Pre-operative preparation\r\n    * Patient assessment and informed consent.\r\n    * General anesthesia is administered.\r\n    * Urinary catheterization may be performed.\r\n2. Abdominal access\r\n    * Open, laparoscopic or robotic approach may be used.\r\n    * Abdomen and pelvis are exposed.\r\n3. Exploration\r\n    * Pelvic and abdominal structures are inspected.\r\n    * Lymph nodes are assessed.\r\n4. Pelvic lymph-node dissection\r\n    * Appropriate pelvic lymph nodes are removed for staging and treatment.\r\n5. Mobilization of bladder\r\n    * Bladder is separated from surrounding tissues.\r\n6. Division of ureters\r\n    * Ureters are identified, divided and prepared for urinary diversion.\r\n7. Removal of bladder\r\n    * Bladder is removed.\r\n    * In men, prostate and seminal vesicles are usually removed with radical cystectomy.\r\n    * In women, removal may include uterus, ovaries, fallopian tubes and part of the vagina depending on the operation. \r\n8. Urinary diversion\r\n    * A new route for urine is created.\r\n    * Common option: ileal conduit.\r\n    * Other options include continent diversion or neobladder in selected patients.\r\n9. Hemostasis\r\n    * Bleeding is controlled.\r\n    * Drains may be placed when required.\r\n10. Closure\r\n\r\n* Abdominal wound is closed in layers.\r\n\r\nFlowchart\r\n\r\nAnesthesia → Abdominal access → Lymph-node dissection → Mobilize bladder → Divide ureters → Remove bladder ± adjacent organs → Urinary diversion → Hemostasis → Closure\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. APPENDECTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Appendectomy is the surgical removal of the vermiform appendix.\r\n* It is most commonly performed for acute appendicitis. \r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Acute appendicitis.\r\n* Perforated appendicitis.\r\n* Gangrenous appendix.\r\n* Appendicitis with generalized peritonitis.\r\n* Recurrent appendicitis in selected cases.\r\n* Appendiceal neoplasm.\r\n* Complicated appendicitis when operative treatment is indicated. \r\n\r\nSurgical Steps of Laparoscopic Appendectomy\r\n\r\n1. Pre-operative preparation\r\n    * Patient assessment and consent.\r\n    * IV fluids and antibiotics as indicated.\r\n    * General anesthesia.\r\n2. Positioning\r\n    * Patient is placed supine.\r\n    * Abdomen is prepared and draped.\r\n3. Creation of pneumoperitoneum\r\n    * Carbon dioxide is introduced into the abdominal cavity.\r\n4. Insertion of ports\r\n    * Usually three laparoscopic ports are inserted.\r\n5. Identification of appendix\r\n    * Cecum is identified.\r\n    * Appendix is located and mobilized.\r\n6. Division of mesoappendix\r\n    * Mesoappendix and appendicular vessels are divided.\r\n7. Division of appendix\r\n    * Appendix is secured at its base and divided.\r\n8. Removal\r\n    * Appendix is placed in a retrieval bag and removed through a port.\r\n9. Inspection\r\n    * Operative field is inspected for bleeding or contamination.\r\n10. Closure\r\n\r\n* Pneumoperitoneum is released.\r\n* Ports are removed.\r\n* Incisions are closed. \r\n\r\nOpen Appendectomy\r\n\r\n* Right lower abdominal incision is made.\r\n* Abdominal wall is opened.\r\n* Appendix is identified.\r\n* Mesoappendix is divided.\r\n* Appendix is ligated and removed.\r\n* Abdominal wound is closed. \r\n\r\nFlowchart\r\n\r\nAnesthesia → Pneumoperitoneum → Ports → Identify appendix → Divide mesoappendix → Ligate/divide appendix → Remove appendix → Check hemostasis → Close wounds\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. CHOLECYSTECTOMY\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Cholecystectomy is the surgical removal of the gallbladder.\r\n* It is most commonly performed laparoscopically. \r\n\r\nIndications\r\n\r\n* Symptomatic gallstones (cholelithiasis).\r\n* Acute cholecystitis.\r\n* Chronic cholecystitis.\r\n* Biliary dyskinesia.\r\n* Acalculous cholecystitis.\r\n* Gallstone pancreatitis.\r\n* Selected gallbladder polyps or masses.\r\n* Complications of gallstone disease. \r\n\r\nSurgical Steps of Laparoscopic Cholecystectomy\r\n\r\n1. Pre-operative preparation\r\n    * Patient assessment and consent.\r\n    * Fasting as instructed.\r\n    * General anesthesia.\r\n2. Positioning\r\n    * Patient is positioned appropriately.\r\n    * Abdomen is prepared and draped.\r\n3. Creation of pneumoperitoneum\r\n    * Carbon dioxide is introduced into the abdominal cavity.\r\n4. Insertion of ports\r\n    * Laparoscope and surgical instruments are introduced through small abdominal incisions.\r\n5. Exposure of gallbladder\r\n    * Gallbladder is grasped and retracted to expose the hepatocystic/Calot triangle.\r\n6. Identification of structures\r\n    * Cystic duct and cystic artery are clearly identified.\r\n    * The critical view of safety is obtained before division.\r\n7. Division of cystic duct and artery\r\n    * Structures are clipped/ligated and divided.\r\n8. Dissection of gallbladder\r\n    * Gallbladder is separated from the liver bed.\r\n9. Removal\r\n    * Gallbladder is placed in a retrieval bag and removed through a port.\r\n10. Inspection and closure\r\n\r\n* Hemostasis is checked.\r\n* Abdomen is desufflated.\r\n* Ports are removed.\r\n* Incisions are closed. \r\n\r\nOpen Cholecystectomy\r\n\r\n* Right upper abdominal incision is made, commonly a right subcostal/Kocher incision.\r\n* Gallbladder is exposed.\r\n* Cystic duct and cystic artery are ligated and divided.\r\n* Gallbladder is separated from the liver.\r\n* Gallbladder is removed.\r\n* Wound is closed. \r\n\r\nFlowchart\r\n\r\nAnesthesia → Pneumoperitoneum → Ports → Expose gallbladder → Identify cystic duct & artery → Clip/divide → Separate gallbladder from liver → Remove gallbladder → Hemostasis → Closure///////                    \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-08-25T14:13:56+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
