rns\r\n4. Diabetes mellitus\r\n5. Pressure and tissue ischemia\r\n6. Frostbite\r\n\r\nTypes of Gangrene\r\n\r\n1. Dry gangrene\r\n\r\n* Usually caused by progressive loss of arterial blood supply.\r\n* Tissue becomes dry, shriveled and black.\r\n* Usually develops slowly.\r\n* There is a clear line of demarcation between healthy and dead tissue.\r\n* Infection is initially minimal.\r\n\r\n2. Wet gangrene\r\n\r\n* Occurs when ischemic tissue becomes infected.\r\n* Tissue is swollen, soft and discolored.\r\n* Pus and foul-smelling discharge may be present.\r\n* Spreads rapidly.\r\n* Can lead to sepsi\n<truncated 2504 bytes>\n3. Tuberculosis (TB)—\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nTuberculosis (TB) is a chronic infectious disease caused mainly by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It most commonly affects the lungs (pulmonary TB) but can involve other organs such as lymph nodes, bones, joints, kidneys and the central nervous system.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Etiological Agent\r\n\r\n* Causative organism: Mycobacterium tuberculosis\r\n* It is an acid-fast bacillus (AFB).\r\n* It is transmitted mainly through airborne respiratory droplets/aerosols from a person with infectious pulmonary or laryngeal TB.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Risk Factors\r\n\r\n* Close contact with an infectious TB patient\r\n* Malnutrition\r\n* Overcrowding\r\n* Poor socioeconomic conditions\r\n* Diabetes mellitus\r\n* Immunosuppression\r\n* HIV infection\r\n* Smoking\r\n* Substance abuse\r\n* Poor general health\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Pathophysiology\r\n\r\nInhalation of TB bacilli → bacilli reach alveoli → uptake by macrophages → cell-mediated immune response → granuloma/tubercle formation → latent or active infection.\r\n\r\nIn active disease\n<truncated 38358 bytes>\n4. Abdominal Surgery: Incisions, Common Procedures and Physiotherapy Implications—-\r\n\r\nDefinition of Abdominal Surgery\r\n\r\nAbdominal surgery refers to surgical procedures performed to diagnose, treat, repair, remove or reconstruct structures within the abdominal and pelvic cavities, including the gastrointestinal, hepatobiliary, urinary and reproductive organs.\r\n\r\nAn open abdominal operation (laparotomy) involves an incision through the abdominal wall to access the abdominal cavity, whereas laparoscopic surgery uses several small incisions and a camera. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Common Abdominal Incisions\r\n\r\nThe choice of incision depends on the organ involved, required surgical exposure, urgency of surgery and patient factors. \r\n\r\nA. Midline incision\r\n\r\n* Vertical incision through the linea alba.\r\n* May be upper, lower or full midline.\r\n* Provides rapid and wide access to the abdominal cavity.\r\n* Commonly used in emergency and exploratory laparotomy.\r\n* Easily extended if greater exposure is required. \r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy\n<truncated 36445 bytes>\n\n# Previous Session Summary:\n1. Outstanding User Requests\n- Format the most recently provided medical text (starting with \"Abdominal Surgery: Incisions, Common Procedures and Physiotherapy Implications—-\") into a highly condensed, punctuation-free, continuous block of text according to the strict formatting rules.\n- Status: IMPLEMENTATION.\n\n2. User Knowledge\n- **Strict Formatting Rules:**\n  - Structure: The output MUST be a single continuous block of text with single spacing.\n  - Headings: Make all main topics and subtopics bold (e.g., **Main Topic**).\n  - Lists & Numbers: Keep all numbers (1, 2, 3...) and bullet points (•) inline within the text block. Do not remove them.\n  - Dividers: Use exactly three dashes (---) as a divider to separate main topics (usually replacing the long dash `⸻` found in the source text).\n  - Special Characters: If there is a sequence of forward slashes (e.g., ///////////// or /), you MUST NOT remove them. Keep them exactly where they appear.\n- **Negative Prompting (WHAT NOT TO DO):**\n  - NO PUNCTUATION: DO NOT use any standard punctuation. Remove all periods, commas, colons, semicolons, exclamation marks, question marks, brackets, and apostrophes. \n  - The only non-alphanumeric characters allowed are the numbers, the bullet symbol (•), the dash divider (---), and forward slashes (///////).\n  - NO LINE BREAKS FOR LISTS: DO NOT use carriage returns or line breaks. Everything must wrap as one continuous paragraph block.\n  - NO DATA MANIPULATION: DO NOT summarize, rewrite, or alter the actual written data or medical facts. \n  - NO IMAGES OR MEDIA.\n  - NO CHIT-CHAT: Output only the formatted text. Do not add introductory or concluding remarks.\n\n3. Work Accomplished\n- Successfully applied the exact formatting rules to a provided text on \"Gangrene\".\n- Successfully applied the exact formatting rules to a massive multi-topic batch covering Tuberculosis, Cellulitis, Hospital-Acquired Infections, Post-Operative Complications, Craniotomy, Anesthesia, and Wound Healing.\n\n4. Model Knowledge\n- **Text Substitution Strategy:** To strictly follow the \"only allowed non-alphanumeric characters\" rule while avoiding data manipulation:\n  - Hyphenated words are separated by spaces instead of hyphens (e.g., \"cell-mediated\" becomes \"cell mediated\").\n  - Special symbols like `%` or `&` should be spelled out as \"percent\" and \"and\", or safely removed/replaced by spaces to preserve medical facts (e.g. \"80%\" -> \"80 percent\").\n  - Arrows (e.g., `→`) should be replaced by spaces.\n  - Apostrophes are completely dropped (e.g., \"patient's\" becomes \"patients\").\n  - Subscript numbers (e.g., SpO₂) are converted to standard inline numbers (SpO2).\n- **Heading Dividers:** The long dash character `⸻` found in the source text serves as an excellent anchor point to replace with the required `---` main topic divider. \n\n5. Files and Code\n- No local files viewed or edited. All operations involve processing text directly from the conversation history.\n\n6. Current Work and Next Steps\n- **Current Work:** The user just provided a new large block of text starting with \"Abdominal Su