{"step_index":9,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T08:44:20Z","content":"Abdominal Surgery Incisions Common Procedures and Physiotherapy Implications --- **Definition of Abdominal Surgery** Abdominal surgery refers to surgical procedures performed to diagnose treat repair remove or reconstruct structures within the abdominal and pelvic cavities including the gastrointestinal hepatobiliary urinary and reproductive organs An open abdominal operation laparotomy involves an incision through the abdominal wall to access the abdominal cavity whereas laparoscopic surgery uses several small incisions and a camera --- **1 Common Abdominal Incisions** The choice of incision depends on the organ involved required surgical exposure urgency of surgery and patient factors **A Midline incision** • Vertical incision through the linea alba • May be upper lower or full midline • Provides rapid and wide access to the abdominal cavity • Commonly used in emergency and exploratory laparotomy • Easily extended if greater exposure is required **Physiotherapy relevance** Large midline wounds may cause pain during coughing deep breathing and movement Patients require wound support breathing exercises and gradual mobilization **B Paramedian incision** • Vertical incision placed lateral to the midline • May provide access to selected abdominal organs • Less commonly used today than the midline approach **C Transverse incision** • Horizontal incision across the abdominal wall • May provide good cosmetic results and can follow the direction of abdominal wall structures • Used for selected abdominal procedures **D Pfannenstiel incision** • Curved transverse incision just above the pubic symphysis • Commonly used for pelvic and gynaecological surgery including abdominal hysterectomy • Provides good cosmetic results and relatively limited upper abdominal access **E Subcostal / Kocher incision** • Oblique incision below and parallel to the costal margin • Right subcostal commonly used for gallbladder and liver surgery • Left subcostal may be used for splenic surgery • Bilateral subcostal incisions joined in the midline are called a rooftop incision **F McBurney / Grid iron incision** • Oblique muscle splitting incision in the right lower quadrant • Traditionally associated with open appendectomy **G Lanz / transverse right lower quadrant incision** • Transverse incision in the right lower abdomen • May be used for open appendectomy --- **2 General Physiotherapy Implications of Abdominal Surgery** Abdominal surgery can produce pain reduced ventilation impaired coughing reduced mobility muscle weakness and risk of postoperative complications **Physiotherapy assessment** • Vital signs • Respiratory rate and SpO2 • Breathing pattern • Chest expansion • Cough and sputum • Pain • Wound and surgical precautions • Abdominal distension • ROM and muscle strength • Bed mobility and functional mobility **Physiotherapy management** **A Respiratory physiotherapy** • Deep breathing exercises • Thoracic expansion exercises • Supported coughing • Airway clearance techniques when clinically indicated • Appropriate positioning **B Early mobilization** • Bed mobility • Sitting out of bed • Sit to stand • Transfers • Progressive walking Early mobilization helps reduce complications associated with immobility and supports recovery **C Circulatory exercises** • Ankle pumps • Active limb exercises • Early ambulation These help reduce venous stasis and risk of thromboembolic complications **D Abdominal wound protection** • Teach log rolling for getting in and out of bed • Support the incision during coughing when appropriate • Avoid excessive strain on the healing abdominal wall • Progress activity according to surgical restrictions **E Musculoskeletal rehabilitation** • ROM exercises • Progressive strengthening • Postural exercises • Gait and functional training • Gradual return to activities of daily living --- **3 Common Abdominal and Pelvic Surgical Procedures** **A Herniorrhaphy** **Definition** Herniorrhaphy is the surgical repair of a hernia by returning the protruding tissue to its normal position and repairing/closing the defect in the abdominal wall **Common types** • Inguinal hernia • Femoral hernia • Umbilical hernia • Incisional hernia **Physiotherapy implications** • Pain management through positioning and activity modification • Breathing exercises and supported coughing • Early mobilization • Gentle ROM exercises • Progressive walking • Gradual abdominal and trunk strengthening after adequate healing and according to surgical instructions • Avoid excessive straining or heavy lifting until medically cleared • Patient education regarding safe body mechanics --- **4 Colostomy** **Definition** A colostomy is a surgical procedure in which a portion of the colon is brought through an opening in the abdominal wall to form a stoma allowing feces to pass into an external stoma bag **Types** • Loop colostomy • End colostomy A colostomy may be temporary or permanent depending on the underlying condition **Physiotherapy implications** • Respiratory exercises after surgery • Early mobilization • Avoid direct pressure on the stoma • Teach safe bed mobility and transfers • Gradual strengthening • Improve posture and functional mobility • Educate regarding protection of the stoma during exercise • Avoid excessive abdominal pressure during the early healing period • Coordinate with the stoma care team regarding appliances and activity --- **5 Ileostomy** **Definition** An ileostomy is a surgical procedure in which the ileum the terminal part of the small intestine is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma **Types** • Loop ileostomy • End ileostomy **Physiotherapy implications** • Early mobilization • Breathing exercises • ROM and circulation exercises • Protect the stoma during transfers and exercise • Avoid excessive abdominal pressure during early healing • Gradual strengthening and aerobic activity • Monitor fatigue hydration status and exercise tolerance because ileostomy patients can be susceptible to fluid and electrolyte losses • Patient education regarding safe activity and stoma protection --- **6 Hysterectomy** **Definition** Hysterectomy is the surgical removal of the uterus womb Depending on the procedure the cervix fallopian tubes and ovaries may also be removed **Types** 1 Total hysterectomy 2 Subtotal/supracervical hysterectomy 3 Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo oophorectomy 4 Radical hysterectomy It may be performed abdominally vaginally or laparoscopically **Physiotherapy implications** • Breathing exercises • Early mobilization • Circulatory exercises • Gentle ROM • Postural correction • Gradual abdominal and pelvic rehabilitation • Pelvic floor muscle training when appropriate • Gait and functional training • Avoid heavy lifting until adequate healing and medical clearance • Education regarding safe return to daily activities --- **7 Prostatectomy** **Definition** Prostatectomy is the surgical removal of part or all of the prostate gland It may be performed for conditions such as prostate cancer or severe benign prostatic enlargement depending on the surgical approach **Physiotherapy implications** • Early mobilization • Breathing and circulation exercises • ROM exercises • Gait training • Pelvic floor muscle training to help manage postoperative urinary incontinence when appropriate • Functional strengthening • Patient education regarding activity progression • Avoid excessive strain during early recovery --- **8 Cystectomy** **Definition** Cystectomy is the surgical removal of the urinary bladder most commonly performed as treatment for selected cases of bladder cancer A urinary diversion is generally required after radical cystectomy **Physiotherapy implications** • Respiratory exercises • Early mobilization • Prevention of DVT and complications of immobility • ROM and strengthening • Gait training • Functional rehabilitation • Appropriate pelvic floor rehabilitation where indicated • Education regarding safe movement with urinary drainage systems or stoma appliances • Monitor fatigue and exercise tolerance --- **9 Appendectomy** **Definition** Appendectomy is the surgical removal of the vermiform appendix most commonly performed for acute appendicitis It may be performed by • Open surgery • Laparoscopic surgery The traditional open approach may use a McBurney/Grid iron incision or a transverse right lower quadrant incision **Physiotherapy implications** • Deep breathing exercises • Supported coughing • Early mobilization • Gentle ROM • Gradual walking • Pain relieving positioning • Wound protection • Progressive return to normal activities after uncomplicated healing If perforation or peritonitis has occurred rehabilitation may need to progress more slowly because of greater systemic illness and surgical complexity --- **10 Cholecystectomy** **Definition** Cholecystectomy is the surgical removal of the gallbladder commonly performed for symptomatic gallstones and related gallbladder disease **Approaches** • Laparoscopic cholecystectomy • Open cholecystectomy Open surgery may use a right subcostal/Kocher incision **Physiotherapy implications** • Deep breathing and thoracic expansion exercises • Supported coughing • Appropriate positioning • Early mobilization • Circulatory exercises • ROM exercises • Gradual gait and endurance training • Protect the abdominal wound • Progressive return to ADLs Upper abdominal incisions can be particularly uncomfortable during deep inspiration and coughing so respiratory rehabilitation is important ///////////////////// --- **2 Marks Each** **a DVT Signs and Prophylaxis** **Signs of DVT** • Calf pain and tenderness • Unilateral swelling of the leg • Redness and warmth • Increased calf circumference • Homans sign may be positive but it is not reliable for diagnosis **Prophylaxis** • Early mobilization • Ankle pump exercises • Adequate hydration • Graduated compression stockings when indicated • Intermittent pneumatic compression • Anticoagulants such as LMWH when prescribed --- **b Scar Definition and Types** **Definition** • Scar Fibrous tissue formed during healing that replaces normal tissue after injury **Types** 1 Normal scar • Flat and soft • Example healed surgical incision 2 Hypertrophic scar • Raised and thick • Remains within the original wound margins • Example scar following burns 3 Keloid scar • Excessive raised scar tissue • Extends beyond the original wound margins • Example scar after ear piercing 4 Atrophic scar • Thin and depressed • Example acne or chickenpox scars --- **c Classification of Antibiotics** 1 Cell wall synthesis inhibitors • Penicillins • Cephalosporins • Carbapenems • Vancomycin 2 Protein synthesis inhibitors • Aminoglycosides • Macrolides • Tetracyclines • Clindamycin 3 Nucleic acid synthesis inhibitors • Fluoroquinolones • Rifampicin • Metronidazole 4 Antimetabolites • Sulfonamides • Trimethoprim 5 Cell membrane disruptors • Polymyxins --- **d Sip Feeding vs Tube Feeding** **Sip Feeding** • Feeding is given orally in small sips • Requires adequate swallowing ability • Patient actively participates in feeding • Suitable for patients who can swallow safely • Examples water milk and oral nutritional supplements **Tube Feeding** • Nutrition is given through a feeding tube • Used when oral feeding is unsafe or inadequate • Does not require normal oral swallowing for delivery • Examples • Nasogastric NG tube • Gastrostomy/PEG tube • Jejunostomy tube --- **e Cardiovascular Parameters Monitored During Anesthesia** • Heart rate HR • Blood pressure BP • ECG and cardiac rhythm • Oxygen saturation SpO2 • Peripheral perfusion • Urine output when indicated • Temperature may also be monitored perioperatively **Important for 2 marks** HR BP ECG SpO2 --- **f Loss of Water Clinical Manifestations and Treatment** **Clinical Manifestations** • Thirst • Dry mouth • Dry mucous membranes • Reduced urine output • Dark/concentrated urine • Weakness • Fatigue • Dizziness • Tachycardia • Hypotension • Poor skin turgor • Confusion in severe dehydration **Treatment** • Oral rehydration solution ORS for suitable mild/moderate cases • IV fluids for severe dehydration or shock • Replace ongoing fluid losses • Correct electrolyte abnormalities • Treat the underlying cause • Monitor • Fluid intake/output • Blood pressure • Heart rate • Urine output • Serum electrolytes //////////////////////////////// --- **Compare CT and MRI in Evaluation of Brain Lesions** **Definition** **CT Scan** • Computed Tomography CT is an imaging technique that uses X rays and computer processing to produce cross sectional images of the brain • It is fast and widely available • It is particularly useful in acute emergencies especially suspected intracranial hemorrhage and head trauma **MRI** • Magnetic Resonance Imaging MRI is an imaging technique that uses a strong magnetic field and radiofrequency waves to produce detailed images of the brain • It provides superior soft tissue contrast compared with CT • It is particularly useful for detailed assessment of brain lesions **CT Advantages in Brain Lesions** • Rapid examination • Excellent for acute intracranial hemorrhage • Excellent for skull fractures • Useful in head injury • Useful for detecting mass effect and hydrocephalus • Useful in emergency situations • More suitable for patients who cannot tolerate prolonged imaging **CT Limitations** • Uses ionizing radiation • Lower soft tissue contrast than MRI • Small or subtle lesions may be less conspicuous • Posterior fossa imaging can be affected by bone artifacts • Iodinated contrast may cause allergic reactions or contrast associated complications in susceptible patients --- **MRI Advantages in Brain Lesions** • Provides excellent soft tissue contrast • Better for characterization of brain tumors • Highly sensitive for ischemic stroke particularly with diffusion weighted imaging • Better visualization of • Brainstem • Cerebellum • Posterior fossa • White matter • Cranial nerves • Useful for detecting • Demyelinating lesions • Brain tumors • Inflammatory lesions • Small infarcts • Some infections • No ionizing radiation **MRI Limitations** • Longer examination time • More expensive • Less available in some settings • Sensitive to patient movement • May be difficult for claustrophobic patients • Some implanted medical devices or metallic foreign bodies may make MRI unsafe or require special precautions • Monitoring critically ill patients can be more difficult --- **Clinical Selection** CT is preferred when • Acute head injury is suspected • Intracranial hemorrhage is suspected • Rapid emergency imaging is required • Skull fracture needs assessment • MRI is contraindicated or unavailable MRI is preferred when • Detailed characterization of a brain lesion is required • Brain tumor is suspected • Small or subtle lesions need evaluation • Demyelinating disease is suspected • Early ischemic stroke needs sensitive imaging • Posterior fossa or brainstem pathology is suspected --- **Physiotherapy Relevance** • Imaging findings help physiotherapists understand the location and extent of neurological lesions • CT may identify hemorrhage mass effect or structural injury • MRI can provide detailed information about areas responsible for • Motor function • Sensation • Coordination • Balance • Speech • Physiotherapy assessment and treatment should be based on the patients clinical neurological findings not imaging alone • In acute brain lesions monitor • Level of consciousness • Vital signs • Motor function • Tone • Sensation • Balance • Functional mobility ////////////////////////// --- **Postural Advice Following Abdominal** **Definition** • Postural advice following abdominal surgery refers to teaching the patient safe positions and movement strategies that reduce pain protect the surgical wound improve breathing and circulation and facilitate early functional recovery **Aims of Postural Advice** • Reduce pain and discomfort • Protect the abdominal incision • Improve lung expansion • Facilitate effective coughing • Reduce risk of pressure injuries • Promote venous circulation • Prevent stiffness and contractures • Facilitate early mobilization and independence **Postural Advice** 1 Semi Fowlers position • Elevate the head and trunk approximately 30 45 degrees • Support the knees with a pillow if comfortable • Reduces tension on the abdominal wound • Facilitates diaphragmatic breathing • Useful for resting and breathing exercises 2 Supine position • Keep the patient comfortably supported • Place a pillow under the knees if required • Avoid excessive abdominal stretching • Change position regularly to prevent pressure related complications 3 Side lying position • Encourage side lying when medically permitted • Place a pillow between the knees for comfort • Support the abdomen with a pillow if needed • Helps vary pressure areas and may improve comfort 4 Sitting position • Encourage sitting upright as soon as medically safe • Keep the trunk supported and avoid excessive slumping • Feet should be supported on the floor or footrest • Upright positioning assists ventilation and prepares the patient for mobilization 5 Standing and walking • Progress from sitting to standing when medically stable • Maintain an upright posture • Begin with short distance walking • Gradually increase walking distance and duration • Avoid sudden twisting or excessive trunk strain --- **Advice During Coughing** • Teach the patient to support the abdominal incision with their hands or a pillow • Take a deep breath before coughing • Perform a controlled cough rather than repeated forceful coughing • Splinting can reduce pain and improve confidence during coughing --- **Advice for Getting Out of Bed** **Log rolling technique** 1 Bend the knees 2 Roll onto the side as one unit 3 Keep the shoulders and pelvis moving together 4 Lower the legs over the side of the bed 5 Push up with the arms to sit 6 Avoid sudden abdominal flexion or twisting 7 Stand after achieving balance **Benefits** • Reduces strain on the abdominal incision • Minimizes pain • Promotes safe independence --- **Precautions** • Avoid prolonged bed rest • Avoid excessive abdominal stretching or twisting in the early healing period • Avoid heavy lifting until medically cleared • Avoid positions that significantly increase pain • Change position regularly • Follow the surgeons specific wound and activity restrictions • Monitor for dizziness breathlessness wound pain or other deterioration during mobilization --- **Physiotherapy Role** • Assess posture pain breathing and mobility • Teach appropriate resting positions • Teach supported coughing • Teach bed mobility and log rolling • Encourage early mobilization • Prescribe ROM and circulation exercises • Progress walking and functional activities • Educate the patient about safe posture and gradual return to normal activities //////////////////////// --- **Radical Hysterectomy** **Indications and Surgical** **Definition** • Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure in which the uterus and cervix are removed along with the parametrial/paracervical tissues and a portion of the upper vagina usually with pelvic lymph node assessment or dissection • It is primarily performed for selected cases of early invasive cervical cancer --- **1 Indications** • Early invasive carcinoma of the cervix is the main indication • Selected FIGO stage IA2 cervical cancer • Selected stage IB cervical cancer • Selected stage IIA cervical cancer depending on tumour size spread and patient factors • Tumour confined to the cervix/upper vagina without disease that makes primary surgery unsuitable • Selected patients with small operable cervical tumours who are medically fit for major surgery • Pelvic lymph node assessment/dissection is commonly performed with radical hysterectomy **Important point** • Patients with bulky tumour or radiological lymph node involvement are generally not suitable for primary radical hysterectomy and may be referred for chemoradiation or other appropriate treatment --- **2 Structures Removed** During radical hysterectomy the surgeon generally removes • Uterus • Cervix • Parametrial/paracervical tissues • Upper portion of vagina • Pelvic lymph nodes when lymphadenectomy is indicated • Fallopian tubes and ovaries may or may not be removed depending on age tumour characteristics and clinical indication --- **3 Surgical Steps** **Pre operative preparation** • Patient is placed in the appropriate operative position • General anesthesia is administered • Urinary/Foley catheter is inserted • Abdomen and vagina are prepared and draped • Thromboembolism prophylaxis is provided as appropriate **Step 1 Abdominal entry** • Abdomen is opened traditionally through a vertical midline incision for open surgery • Abdominal and pelvic organs are inspected for metastatic disease • Suspicious lymph nodes or other lesions are assessed **Step 2 Exposure of the pelvis** • Bowel is displaced from the pelvis • Uterus is held with appropriate traction • Pelvic sidewalls are exposed **Step 3 Opening the broad ligament** • Round ligament is identified divided and ligated • Broad ligament is opened • The course of the ureter is identified and protected **Step 4 Pelvic lymphadenectomy** • Pelvic lymph nodes are dissected when indicated • Important structures such as the obturator nerve and vessels are identified and preserved **Step 5 Development of pelvic spaces** • Paravesical space is developed • Pararectal space is developed • These spaces help identify important pelvic structures and facilitate safe dissection **Step 6 Uterine vessels** • Uterine artery and associated vessels are identified • Uterine vessels are ligated and divided near their origin as appropriate for the radical procedure **Step 7 Ureteric dissection** • Ureter is carefully identified • The ureter is dissected from surrounding tissues to allow safe removal of the parametrial tissues • Care is taken to prevent ureteric injury **Step 8 Bladder mobilization** • Bladder is separated and mobilized from the cervix and upper vagina • Vesicouterine ligaments are carefully divided **Step 9 Posterior and lateral dissection** • Uterosacral and rectovaginal/related supporting tissues are divided • Parametrial and paracervical tissues are removed with the uterus and cervix • Nerve sparing techniques may be used where appropriate to reduce postoperative bladder bowel and sexual dysfunction **Step 10 Removal of specimen** • The uterus cervix parametrial tissues and upper vaginal cuff are removed as one specimen • Surgical margins are assessed **Step 11 Vaginal closure** • The vaginal cuff is closed • Hemostasis is checked carefully **Step 12 Closure** • Pelvic and abdominal structures are returned to appropriate positions • Abdomen is closed in layers • Specimens are sent for histopathological examination --- **4 Flowchart of Surgical Steps** Anesthesia catheterization Abdominal incision and exploration Open broad ligament and identify ureters Pelvic lymphadenectomy Develop paravesical and pararectal spaces Ligate uterine vessels Mobilize bladder and dissect ureters Divide parametrial uterosacral and related ligaments Remove uterus cervix parametrium upper vagina Close vaginal cuff Hemostasis and abdominal closure --- **5 Important Complications** • Hemorrhage • Bladder injury • Ureteric injury • Bowel injury • Urinary dysfunction • Lymphocele • Lymphedema • Infection • Venous thromboembolism • Sexual dysfunction • Bladder and bowel dysfunction ///////////////////////////// --- **Herniorrhaphy Colostomy and Ileostomy** **Indications and Surgery step** --- **1 HERNIORRHAPHY** **Definition** • Herniorrhaphy is the surgical repair of a hernia by reducing the herniated contents and repairing the defect in the abdominal wall • In modern practice many hernia repairs use mesh reinforcement depending on the type and characteristics of the hernia **Indications** • Symptomatic hernia causing pain or discomfort • Enlarging hernia • Irreducible/incarcerated hernia • Strangulated hernia • Intestinal obstruction due to hernia • Recurrent hernia • Risk of complications such as obstruction or strangulation • Cosmetic or functional problems in selected patients **Surgical Steps of Open Herniorrhaphy** 1 Pre operative preparation • Patient assessment and consent • General regional or local anesthesia depending on the procedure • Surgical site prepared and draped 2 Incision • Incision is made over the hernia site 3 Exposure • Skin and subcutaneous tissues are dissected • Hernia sac is identified 4 Dissection of hernia sac • Sac is carefully separated from surrounding structures • In inguinal hernia the sac is dissected from the spermatic cord structures when applicable 5 Reduction of contents • Herniated bowel omentum or other tissue is returned to the abdominal cavity • Non viable tissue may require resection 6 Management of hernia sac • Sac is ligated and/or excised as appropriate 7 Repair of abdominal wall defect • Fascial defect is repaired with sutures and/or reinforced with mesh depending on the operation 8 Closure • Hemostasis is achieved • Wound is closed in layers **Flowchart** Incision Identify hernia Dissect sac Reduce contents Manage sac Repair/reinforce defect Wound closure --- **2 COLOSTOMY** **Definition** • Colostomy is a surgical procedure in which a portion of the colon is brought through an opening in the abdominal wall to form a stoma allowing feces to pass into an external appliance **Indications** • Colorectal cancer • Intestinal obstruction • Bowel perforation • Traumatic bowel injury • Diverticular disease with obstruction or perforation • Inflammatory bowel disease • Protection of a distal colorectal/intestinal anastomosis • Severe perineal or anorectal disease • Fecal incontinence in selected cases • Temporary diversion when the distal bowel requires healing • Permanent diversion when the colon/rectum/anus cannot be restored to normal function **Types** • End colostomy • Loop colostomy • Double barrel colostomy • May be temporary or permanent **Surgical Steps of Colostomy** 1 Pre operative preparation • Patient assessment and consent • Stoma site is ideally marked with consideration of body position skin folds scars and bony prominences 2 Anesthesia • Usually performed under general anesthesia 3 Abdominal access • Open or laparoscopic approach may be used 4 Identify the colon • Diseased or damaged segment is identified • Bowel may be resected if required 5 Select the stoma site • An opening is created through the abdominal wall commonly through the rectus muscle 6 Bring colon through abdominal wall • The selected segment of colon is delivered through the stoma opening without tension or twisting 7 Create the stoma • The colon is opened and its edges are sutured to the surrounding skin • The stoma is matured 8 Closure • Abdominal incision is closed • Ostomy pouch is applied **Flowchart** Anesthesia Abdominal access Identify colon Resect/bypass diseased segment Create abdominal opening Bring colon out Mature stoma Close wound Apply pouch --- **3 ILEOSTOMY** **Definition** • Ileostomy is a surgical procedure in which the ileum is brought through the abdominal wall to form a stoma allowing intestinal contents to leave the body through the stoma **Indications** • Ulcerative colitis • Crohns disease involving the colon • Colorectal cancer • Total or subtotal colectomy • Proctocolectomy • Intestinal obstruction • Severe bowel injury • Bowel perforation • Protection of a distal intestinal anastomosis • Temporary fecal diversion • Complicated colorectal surgery Ileostomy may be temporary or permanent depending on the underlying disease and whether bowel continuity can later be restored **Types** • End ileostomy • Loop ileostomy • Continent ileostomy in selected cases **Surgical Steps of Ileostomy** 1 Pre operative preparation • Patient assessment and consent • Stoma site is selected and marked 2 Anesthesia • Usually performed under general anesthesia 3 Abdominal access • Open or laparoscopic approach may be used 4 Identify the ileum • Terminal ileum is identified • Adequate blood supply and mobility are confirmed 5 Resection if required • Diseased bowel may be removed according to the primary operation 6 Create stoma opening • An opening is created through the abdominal wall 7 Bring ileum through the abdominal wall • Ileal segment is brought out without twisting or excessive tension 8 Mature the ileostomy • Ileum is opened and sutured to the skin • An ileostomy is generally everted to form a spout 9 Closure • Abdominal wound is closed • Ileostomy appliance is fitted **Flowchart** Anesthesia Abdominal access Identify ileum Resect diseased bowel if required Create stoma opening Bring ileum out Evert/mature stoma Close wound Apply pouch /////////////////// --- **Cystectomy Appendectomy and Cholecystectomy** **Indications and Surgical Steps** --- **1 CYSTECTOMY** **Definition** • Cystectomy is the surgical removal of the urinary bladder either partially or completely • Partial cystectomy only part of the bladder is removed • Radical cystectomy the entire bladder is removed usually with pelvic lymph node dissection and urinary diversion **Indications** • Muscle invasive bladder cancer • High risk or recurrent bladder cancer not adequately controlled by other treatment • Extensive bladder tumour involving multiple areas • Selected localized bladder tumours suitable for partial cystectomy • Severe bladder disease in selected cases • Palliative removal in selected patients with uncontrolled bleeding pain or fistula **Surgical Steps of Radical Cystectomy** 1 Pre operative preparation • Patient assessment and informed consent • General anesthesia is administered • Urinary catheterization may be performed 2 Abdominal access • Open laparoscopic or robotic approach may be used • Abdomen and pelvis are exposed 3 Exploration • Pelvic and abdominal structures are inspected • Lymph nodes are assessed 4 Pelvic lymph node dissection • Appropriate pelvic lymph nodes are removed for staging and treatment 5 Mobilization of bladder • Bladder is separated from surrounding tissues 6 Division of ureters • Ureters are identified divided and prepared for urinary diversion 7 Removal of bladder • Bladder is removed • In men prostate and seminal vesicles are usually removed with radical cystectomy • In women removal may include uterus ovaries fallopian tubes and part of the vagina depending on the operation 8 Urinary diversion • A new route for urine is created • Common option ileal conduit • Other options include continent diversion or neobladder in selected patients 9 Hemostasis • Bleeding is controlled • Drains may be placed when required 10 Closure • Abdominal wound is closed in layers **Flowchart** Anesthesia Abdominal access Lymph node dissection Mobilize bladder Divide ureters Remove bladder adjacent organs Urinary diversion Hemostasis Closure --- **2 APPENDECTOMY** **Definition** • Appendectomy is the surgical removal of the vermiform appendix • It is most commonly performed for acute appendicitis **Indications** • Acute appendicitis • Perforated appendicitis • Gangrenous appendix • Appendicitis with generalized peritonitis • Recurrent appendicitis in selected cases • Appendiceal neoplasm • Complicated appendicitis when operative treatment is indicated **Surgical Steps of Laparoscopic Appendectomy** 1 Pre operative preparation • Patient assessment and consent • IV fluids and antibiotics as indicated • General anesthesia 2 Positioning • Patient is placed supine • Abdomen is prepared and draped 3 Creation of pneumoperitoneum • Carbon dioxide is introduced into the abdominal cavity 4 Insertion of ports • Usually three laparoscopic ports are inserted 5 Identification of appendix • Cecum is identified • Appendix is located and mobilized 6 Division of mesoappendix • Mesoappendix and appendicular vessels are divided 7 Division of appendix • Appendix is secured at its base and divided 8 Removal • Appendix is placed in a retrieval bag and removed through a port 9 Inspection • Operative field is inspected for bleeding or contamination 10 Closure • Pneumoperitoneum is released • Ports are removed • Incisions are closed **Open Appendectomy** • Right lower abdominal incision is made • Abdominal wall is opened • Appendix is identified • Mesoappendix is divided • Appendix is ligated and removed • Abdominal wound is closed **Flowchart** Anesthesia Pneumoperitoneum Ports Identify appendix Divide mesoappendix Ligate/divide appendix Remove appendix Check hemostasis Close wounds --- **3 CHOLECYSTECTOMY** **Definition** • Cholecystectomy is the surgical removal of the gallbladder • It is most commonly performed laparoscopically **Indications** • Symptomatic gallstones cholelithiasis • Acute cholecystitis • Chronic cholecystitis • Biliary dyskinesia • Acalculous cholecystitis • Gallstone pancreatitis • Selected gallbladder polyps or masses • Complications of gallstone disease **Surgical Steps of Laparoscopic Cholecystectomy** 1 Pre operative preparation • Patient assessment and consent • Fasting as instructed • General anesthesia 2 Positioning • Patient is positioned appropriately • Abdomen is prepared and draped 3 Creation of pneumoperitoneum • Carbon dioxide is introduced into the abdominal cavity 4 Insertion of ports • Laparoscope and surgical instruments are introduced through small abdominal incisions 5 Exposure of gallbladder • Gallbladder is grasped and retracted to expose the hepatocystic/Calot triangle 6 Identification of structures • Cystic duct and cystic artery are clearly identified • The critical view of safety is obtained before division 7 Division of cystic duct and artery • Structures are clipped/ligated and divided 8 Dissection of gallbladder • Gallbladder is separated from the liver bed 9 Removal • Gallbladder is placed in a 