{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T11:25:23Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n1. DVT – Deep Vein Thrombosis\r\n\r\n* Definition: Formation of a blood clot (thrombus) in a deep vein, usually of the lower limb.\r\n* Signs: Unilateral swelling, calf pain/tenderness, warmth and redness.\r\n* Complication: Pulmonary embolism.\r\n* Prevention: Early mobilization, ankle-pump exercises, compression devices and anticoagulants when prescribed.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Scar + Types\r\n\r\n* Definition: A scar is fibrous connective tissue formed during healing after tissue injury.\r\n* Types:\r\n    * Normal scar: Flat, soft and within wound margins.\r\n    * Hypertrophic scar: Raised but remains within wound margins.\r\n    * Keloid: Raised scar extending beyond original wound margins.\r\n    * Atrophic scar: Thin, depressed scar.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Types of Antibiotics\r\n\r\n* Cell-wall inhibitors: Penicillins, cephalosporins.\r\n* Protein-synthesis inhibitors: Aminoglycosides, macrolides, tetracyclines.\r\n* Nucleic-acid inhibitors: Fluoroquinolones, rifampicin.\r\n* Antimetabolites: Sulfonamides, trimethoprim.\r\n* Cell-membrane disruptors: Polymyxins.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Dehydration + Manifestations\r\n\r\n* Definition: Dehydration is a condition in which body water loss exceeds water intake.\r\n* Manifestations:\r\n    * Thirst.\r\n    * Dry mouth and mucous membranes.\r\n    * Reduced/dark urine.\r\n    * Weakness and dizziness.\r\n    * Tachycardia.\r\n    * Hypotension in severe cases.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Surgical Drains\r\n\r\n* Definition: Surgical drains are tubes/devices used to remove blood, pus, serous fluid or other unwanted collections from a wound or body cavity.\r\n* Examples:\r\n    * Intercostal drain (ICD).\r\n    * Hemovac.\r\n    * Jackson-Pratt drain.\r\n    * T-tube.\r\n* Purpose: Prevent fluid accumulation and facilitate healing.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Types of Anaesthesia\r\n\r\n* Local anaesthesia: Loss of sensation in a limited area without loss of consciousness.\r\n* Regional anaesthesia: Blocks sensation in a larger region.\r\n    * Example: spinal, epidural.\r\n* General anaesthesia: Drug-induced reversible unconsciousness with loss of protective reflexes and sensation.\r\n* Sedation: Reduced anxiety/awareness with varying levels of consciousness.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n7. Types of Wounds\r\n\r\n* Open wound: Break in skin continuity.\r\n    * Example: laceration, abrasion.\r\n* Closed wound: Skin remains intact but underlying tissue is damaged.\r\n    * Example: contusion.\r\n* Acute wound: Usually heals in an expected time.\r\n    * Example: surgical incision.\r\n* Chronic/non-healing wound: Fails to progress normally through healing.\r\n    * Example: diabetic or pressure ulcer.\r\n* Surgical wounds may also be classified as clean, clean-contaminated, contaminated and dirty/infected.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n8. Wound Healing\r\n\r\n* Definition: Wound healing is the biological process by which the body repairs damaged tissue and restores tissue continuity.\r\n* Phases:\r\n    1. Hemostasis – stops bleeding.\r\n    2. Inflammation – removes bacteria and debris.\r\n    3. Proliferation – granulation tissue, collagen, angiogenesis and epithelialization.\r\n    4. Remodeling – collagen reorganization and scar maturation. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n9. Gangrene\r\n\r\n* Definition: Gangrene is tissue necrosis caused by inadequate blood supply, infection, or both.\r\n* Types:\r\n    * Dry gangrene: Usually due to ischemia.\r\n    * Wet gangrene: Necrotic tissue with infection.\r\n    * Gas gangrene: Rapidly progressive infection with gas formation. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n10. Cellulitis\r\n\r\n* Definition: Cellulitis is an acute bacterial infection of the skin and deeper subcutaneous tissues.\r\n* Clinical features:\r\n    * Redness.\r\n    * Warmth.\r\n    * Swelling.\r\n    * Pain/tenderness.\r\n    * Fever may occur.\r\n* Treatment: Antibiotics, elevation and treatment of the underlying cause.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n11. Abscess\r\n\r\n* Definition: An abscess is a localized collection of pus within a tissue or body cavity, usually caused by infection.\r\n* Clinical features:\r\n    * Pain.\r\n    * Swelling.\r\n    * Redness.\r\n    * Warmth.\r\n    * Fluctuant/pus-filled swelling.\r\n    * Fever may occur.\r\n* Treatment: Drainage when indicated + appropriate antibiotics.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n12. Amputation\r\n\r\n* Definition: Amputation is the surgical removal of part or all of a limb.\r\n* Indications: Severe trauma, gangrene, peripheral vascular disease, uncontrolled infection and malignancy.\r\n* Types: Above-knee, below-knee, above-elbow and below-elbow amputations.\r\n* Rehabilitation: Stump care, strengthening, ROM, prosthetic training and gait training.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n13. Phantom Limb\r\n\r\n* Definition: Phantom limb sensation is the feeling that an amputated limb is still present.\r\n* Phantom limb pain: Pain perceived in the missing limb.\r\n* Features: Burning, tingling, shooting pain or sensation of movement.\r\n* Management: Desensitization, mirror therapy, graded motor imagery, appropriate prosthetic rehabilitation and medical pain management.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n14. Varicose Veins\r\n\r\n* Definition: Varicose veins are dilated, elongated and tortuous superficial veins, usually of the lower limbs, caused mainly by venous valve incompetence.\r\n* Features: Visible twisted veins, aching, heaviness, ankle edema and skin changes.\r\n* Management: Exercise, leg elevation, compression when appropriate and selected surgical/endovenous treatment.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n15. Craniotomy\r\n\r\n* Definition: Craniotomy is a neurosurgical procedure in which a portion of the skull is temporarily removed to provide access to the brain and is usually replaced after surgery.\r\n* Indications: Brain tumour, aneurysm, hematoma, vascular lesions and other intracranial conditions.\r\n* Difference: In craniectomy, the bone flap is not immediately replaced.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n16. Laminectomy\r\n\r\n* Definition: Laminectomy is a spinal operation involving removal of part or all of the vertebral lamina to decompress the spinal cord or nerve roots.\r\n* Indications: Spinal stenosis, nerve-root compression, selected disc disease and spinal tumours.\r\n* Complications: Dural tear/CSF leak, infection, bleeding, neurological injury and spinal instability. 18. Postoperative Complications\r\n\r\n* Definition: Postoperative complications are unwanted conditions occurring after surgery that may delay recovery or cause morbidity.\r\n* Common complications:\r\n    * Shock.\r\n    * DVT.\r\n    * Wound infection.\r\n    * Atelectasis.\r\n    * Pneumonia.\r\n    * UTI.\r\n    * Pressure injuries.\r\n    * Hemorrhage.\r\n    * Wound dehiscence.\r\n    * Ileus.\r\n* Physiotherapy prevention: Early mobilization, breathing exercises, circulation exercises, ROM, positioning and functional rehabilitation. /////////////////////// Craniotomy: Overview, Indications and Complications- \r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\nA craniotomy is a neurosurgical procedure in which a portion of the skull is temporarily removed as a bone flap to provide access to the brain for diagnosis or treatment; the bone flap is usually replaced at the end of surgery.1. Overview of Craniotomy\r\n\r\nA craniotomy provides the surgeon with access to intracranial structures while attempting to minimize damage to normal brain tissue. The location and size of the craniotomy depend on the pathology being treated.\r\n\r\nCommon types according to location\r\n\r\n* Frontal craniotomy\r\n* Temporal craniotomy\r\n* Parietal craniotomy\r\n* Occipital craniotomy\r\n* Pterional craniotomy\r\n* Retrosigmoid/suboccipital craniotomy\r\n\r\nIt may be performed under general anesthesia, while selected procedures involving areas responsible for speech or movement may be performed as an awake craniotomy to allow neurological monitoring during surgery. \r\n\r\nBasic procedure\r\n\r\n1. Patient is positioned and anesthetized.\r\n2. A scalp incision is made.\r\n3. Burr holes are created.\r\n4. A section of skull is removed as a bone flap.\r\n5. The dura is opened to expose the brain.\r\n6. The required intracranial procedure is performed.\r\n7. The dura is closed.\r\n8. The bone flap is replaced and fixed.\r\n9. The scalp is closed. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Indications of Craniotomy\r\n\r\nCraniotomy may be performed for diagnostic, therapeutic or decompressive purposes.\r\n\r\nA. Brain tumours\r\n\r\n* Meningioma\r\n* Glioma\r\n* Metastatic brain tumour\r\n* Pituitary and parasellar lesions\r\n* Other intracranial tumours\r\n\r\nB. Cerebrovascular conditions\r\n\r\n* Cerebral aneurysm\r\n* Arteriovenous malformation (AVM)\r\n* Intracerebral hemorrhage\r\n* Selected vascular malformations\r\n\r\nC. Traumatic brain injury\r\n\r\n* Epidural hematoma\r\n* Acute subdural hematoma\r\n* Depressed skull fracture\r\n* Intracranial foreign body\r\n\r\nD. Raised intracranial pressure / brain swelling\r\n\r\n* Selected cases of severe cerebral edema or swelling following trauma or stroke.\r\n\r\nE. Infectious lesions\r\n\r\n* Brain abscess\r\n* Subdural empyema\r\n\r\nF. Other indications\r\n\r\n* Epilepsy surgery to remove a seizure focus\r\n* Microvascular decompression for selected cranial nerve disorders\r\n* Biopsy or removal of intracranial lesions\r\n* Selected functional neurosurgical procedures. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Complications of Craniotomy\r\n\r\nComplications can be neurological, surgical, systemic or wound-related.\r\n\r\nA. Neurological complications\r\n\r\n* Brain injury\r\n* New neurological deficit\r\n* Limb weakness or paralysis\r\n* Sensory impairment\r\n* Speech or language disturbance\r\n* Visual disturbance\r\n* Problems with balance and coordination\r\n* Cognitive or memory changes\r\n* Seizures\r\n* Stroke\r\n* Cerebral edema\r\n\r\nB. Hemorrhagic complications\r\n\r\n* Intracranial hemorrhage\r\n* Epidural or subdural hematoma\r\n* Excessive blood loss\r\n* Venous sinus injury\r\n\r\nC. CSF-related complications\r\n\r\n* Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leak\r\n* Hydrocephalus\r\n* Pneumocephalus\r\n\r\nD. Infectious complications\r\n\r\n* Surgical-site infection\r\n* Meningitis\r\n* Bone-flap infection\r\n* Brain abscess or empyema\r\n\r\nE. Other complications\r\n\r\n* Headache\r\n* Nausea and vomiting\r\n* Scalp numbness or pain\r\n* Cosmetic deformity\r\n* Temporalis muscle weakness/atrophy\r\n* Deep-vein thrombosis and other complications related to immobility. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Physiotherapy Implications\r\n\r\nAlthough the question focuses on craniotomy, this section is useful for a physiotherapy university answer.\r\n\r\nAssessment\r\n\r\nThe physiotherapist should assess:\r\n\r\n* Level of consciousness\r\n* Motor power\r\n* Muscle tone\r\n* Sensation\r\n* Balance and coordination\r\n* Cranial nerve-related deficits\r\n* Respiratory status\r\n* Functional mobility\r\n* Activities of daily living\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy management\r\n\r\n* Appropriate positioning and pressure-area care.\r\n* Respiratory assessment and treatment when clinically indicated.\r\n* Passive/active ROM exercises.\r\n* Prevention of contractures and muscle weakness.\r\n* Early and progressive mobilization when medically stable.\r\n* Bed mobility and transfer training.\r\n* Sitting and standing balance exercises.\r\n* Gait training.\r\n* Progressive strengthening and endurance training.\r\n* Functional and ADL rehabilitation.\r\n* Patient and caregiver education.\r\n\r\nTreatment must be modified according to neurological status, surgical precautions, intracranial pressure concerns and medical stability.//////////////////////// Decompressive Craniectomy in Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI)-\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Decompressive craniectomy (DC) is a neurosurgical procedure in which a large portion of the skull is removed and the bone flap is not immediately replaced, creating additional intracranial space to reduce raised intracranial pressure (ICP) and prevent or treat brain herniation. \r\n* It may be performed as part of the surgical treatment of severe traumatic brain injury, particularly when intracranial hypertension remains refractory to appropriate medical treatment. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Objectives\r\n\r\n* Reduce intracranial pressure (ICP).\r\n* Increase intracranial compliance.\r\n* Improve cerebral perfusion.\r\n* Reduce secondary brain injury.\r\n* Prevent cerebral herniation.\r\n* Prevent further neurological deterioration.\r\n* Reduce mortality in appropriately selected patients. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Indications in TBI\r\n\r\n* Severe TBI with refractory intracranial hypertension despite appropriate medical treatment.\r\n* Severe cerebral edema with raised ICP.\r\n* Significant traumatic brain swelling.\r\n* Acute subdural hematoma with severe brain swelling or brain bulging after evacuation.\r\n* Intracranial hematoma associated with significant mass effect.\r\n* Neurological deterioration due to raised ICP.\r\n* Clinical or radiological evidence of impending brain herniation.\r\n* Diffuse cerebral edema with medically refractory intracranial hypertension. \r\n\r\nImportant examination point\r\n\r\n* Secondary decompressive craniectomy for late refractory ICP elevation is recommended to improve mortality and favorable outcomes.\r\n* Current guidelines do not recommend routine early secondary DC simply for modest ICP elevation that is still medically manageable. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Types\r\n\r\nA. Primary decompressive craniectomy\r\n\r\n* Performed during the initial surgery for a traumatic mass lesion.\r\n* The bone flap is left out after evacuation of the hematoma or other traumatic lesion.\r\n* Commonly considered with severe acute subdural hematoma and significant cerebral swelling. \r\n\r\nB. Secondary decompressive craniectomy\r\n\r\n* Performed later when intracranial pressure remains refractory to medical treatment.\r\n* May be unilateral or bifrontal depending on the pattern of brain swelling. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Surgical Steps\r\n\r\n1. General anesthesia\r\n    * Patient is anesthetized and appropriately positioned.\r\n2. Scalp incision\r\n    * A large scalp incision is made according to the planned decompression.\r\n3. Creation of burr holes\r\n    * Multiple burr holes are created in the skull.\r\n4. Removal of large bone flap\r\n    * A large portion of the skull is removed.\r\n    * In severe unilateral injury, a large frontotemporoparietal decompression may be performed. \r\n5. Evacuation of traumatic lesion\r\n    * Hematoma such as subdural or epidural hematoma may be evacuated when indicated.\r\n6. Opening of dura\r\n    * The dura mater is opened widely to allow the swollen brain to expand.\r\n7. Duraplasty\r\n    * The dura may be enlarged using a graft to provide additional space.\r\n8. Hemostasis\r\n    * Bleeding is controlled and the brain is carefully inspected.\r\n9. Bone flap not replaced\r\n    * Unlike craniotomy, the removed bone flap is not immediately replaced.\r\n10. Closure\r\n\r\n* The scalp is closed over the decompressed area.\r\n\r\n11. Later cranioplasty\r\n\r\n* After the acute phase has resolved, the skull defect is usually reconstructed by cranioplasty when appropriate. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n5. Complications\r\n\r\nEarly complications\r\n\r\n* Intracranial bleeding or hematoma expansion.\r\n* Cerebral edema.\r\n* External cerebral herniation.\r\n* CSF leak.\r\n* Subdural hygroma.\r\n* Wound infection.\r\n* Seizures.\r\n* Hydrocephalus.\r\n* Contralateral hematoma. \r\n\r\nLate complications\r\n\r\n* Syndrome of the trephined/sinking skin flap syndrome.\r\n* Hydrocephalus.\r\n* Persistent subdural collections.\r\n* Neurological deterioration.\r\n* Cosmetic skull defect.\r\n* Complications related to subsequent cranioplasty. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n6. Physiotherapy Implications\r\n\r\nAssessment\r\n\r\n* Level of consciousness/GCS.\r\n* Pupillary response as documented by the medical team.\r\n* Motor power.\r\n* Muscle tone.\r\n* Sensory function.\r\n* Balance and coordination.\r\n* Respiratory status.\r\n* Vital signs and SpO₂.\r\n* Functional mobility.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy management\r\n\r\n* Proper positioning and pressure-area care.\r\n* Respiratory assessment and treatment when indicated.\r\n* Passive and active ROM exercises.\r\n* Prevention of contractures.\r\n* Prevention of pressure injuries.\r\n* Gradual mobilization when medically stable.\r\n* Bed mobility and transfer training.\r\n* Sitting and standing balance exercises.\r\n* Gait training.\r\n* Progressive strengthening and functional rehabilitation.\r\n* Family/caregiver education.\r\n\r\nPrecautions\r\n\r\n* Avoid pressure or trauma to the craniectomy defect.\r\n* Appropriate head protection should be used according to neurosurgical recommendations.\r\n* Avoid treatment that may significantly increase ICP.\r\n* Monitor neurological status and vital signs closely.\r\n* Stop treatment and seek medical review if there is acute neurological deterioration, vomiting, reduced consciousness, seizures or significant cardiorespiratory instability./////////////////////Laminectomy\r\n\r\nDefinition, Indications, Procedure and Complications-\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Laminectomy is a spinal surgical procedure in which part or all of the lamina of a vertebra is removed to decompress the spinal cord or nerve roots.\r\n* It is commonly performed to relieve pressure caused by spinal stenosis, disc disease, tumors or other compressive lesions.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Indications\r\n\r\nLaminectomy may be indicated in:\r\n\r\n* Spinal canal stenosis causing neurological symptoms.\r\n* Compression of spinal cord or nerve roots.\r\n* Herniated intervertebral disc in selected cases.\r\n* Severe radiculopathy or sciatica due to compression.\r\n* Spinal tumors.\r\n* Epidural abscess or other space-occupying lesions.\r\n* Traumatic spinal cord compression.\r\n* Some cases of degenerative spinal disease.\r\n* Cauda equina compression when urgent decompression is required.\r\n* Persistent neurological symptoms not responding adequately to conservative treatment.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n2. Procedure / Surgical Steps\r\n\r\nA. Pre-operative preparation\r\n\r\n* Patient assessment and informed consent.\r\n* Appropriate imaging such as X-ray, CT or MRI.\r\n* General anesthesia is usually administered.\r\n* Patient is positioned prone on the operating table.\r\n\r\nB. Surgical procedure\r\n\r\n1. Skin incision\r\n    * A midline incision is made over the affected vertebral levels.\r\n2. Exposure\r\n    * Paraspinal muscles are carefully separated from the vertebral structures.\r\n    * The lamina and spinous processes are exposed.\r\n3. Identification of affected level\r\n    * The surgeon confirms the appropriate spinal level.\r\n4. Removal of lamina\r\n    * Part or all of the lamina is removed using appropriate surgical instruments.\r\n    * This creates additional space within the spinal canal.\r\n5. Decompression\r\n    * Compressed spinal cord or nerve roots are decompressed.\r\n    * Additional procedures such as discectomy or foraminotomy may be performed when indicated.\r\n6. Hemostasis\r\n    * Bleeding is controlled.\r\n7. Closure\r\n    * Muscles and soft tissues are returned to position.\r\n    * The wound is closed in layers.\r\n8. Post-operative care\r\n    * Neurological status, pain, wound condition and mobility are monitored.\r\n    * Early mobilization is encouraged when medically appropriate.\r\n\r\nFlowchart\r\n\r\nAnesthesia → Prone position → Skin incision → Muscle exposure → Identify vertebral level → Remove lamina → Decompress neural structures → Hemostasis → Wound closure → Rehabilitation\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n3. Complications\r\n\r\nA. Neurological complications\r\n\r\n* Nerve-root injury.\r\n* Spinal cord injury.\r\n* New weakness or paralysis.\r\n* Sensory disturbances.\r\n* Persistent or recurrent radicular pain.\r\n\r\nB. Surgical complications\r\n\r\n* Bleeding/hematoma.\r\n* Infection.\r\n* Dural tear and CSF leak.\r\n* Wound problems.\r\n* Injury to surrounding tissues.\r\n\r\nC. Musculoskeletal complications\r\n\r\n* Spinal instability.\r\n* Post-operative back pain.\r\n* Muscle weakness.\r\n* Reduced spinal mobility.\r\n* Recurrent spinal stenosis.\r\n* Adjacent segment degeneration in some patients.\r\n\r\nD. Other complications\r\n\r\n* Deep-vein thrombosis.\r\n* Pulmonary complications related to prolonged immobility.\r\n* Need for further spinal surgery.\r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n4. Physiotherapy Implications\r\n\r\nAlthough not specifically requested, this earns useful marks in a physiotherapy surgery exam.\r\n\r\nAssessment\r\n\r\n* Pain and neurological symptoms.\r\n* Muscle strength.\r\n* Sensory status.\r\n* ROM.\r\n* Posture.\r\n* Balance.\r\n* Functional mobility.\r\n* Gait.\r\n* Respiratory and cardiovascular status.\r\n\r\nPhysiotherapy management\r\n\r\n* Proper positioning.\r\n* Breathing and circulation exercises.\r\n* Early mobilization when medically stable.\r\n* Bed mobility and transfer training.\r\n* Gentle ROM exercises.\r\n* Progressive strengthening.\r\n* Gait training.\r\n* Postural education.\r\n* Gradual functional rehabilitation.\r\n* Patient education regarding spinal precautions and safe body mechanics.\r\n\r\nPrecautions\r\n\r\n* Avoid excessive spinal flexion, extension, rotation or loading during the early healing period according to surgical instructions.\r\n* Avoid heavy lifting until medically cleared.\r\n* Protect the surgical wound.\r\n* Monitor for new weakness, sensory changes, increasing pain, fever or wound discharge and report appropriately.//////////////////////Aneurysm Clipping vs Coiling—\r\n\r\nDefinition of Cerebral Aneurysm\r\n\r\n* A cerebral aneurysm is a localized abnormal dilatation or ballooning of a blood vessel in the brain due to weakness of the arterial wall.\r\n* Rupture can cause subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH), which is a neurological emergency. \r\n\r\n⸻\r\n\r\n1. Aneurysm Clipping\r\n\r\nDefinition\r\n\r\n* Surgical clipping is an open neurosurgical procedure in which a small metal clip is placed across the neck of the aneurysm to prevent blood from entering the aneurysm. \r\n\r\nSurgical Steps\r\n\r\n1. General anesthesia is given.\r\n2. A scalp incision is made.\r\n3. A craniotomy is performed.\r\n4. The surgeon exposes the aneurysm.\r\n5. The parent artery and aneurysm neck are identified.\r\n6. A small metal clip is placed across the aneurysm neck.\r\n7. Blood flow into the aneurysm is stopped.\r\n8. Hemostasis is achieved.\r\n9. The bone flap is replaced and the wound is closed.\r\n\r\nAdvantages\r\n\r\n* Provides direct visualization of the aneurysm.\r\n* Usually provides durable occlusion.\r\n* Lower risk of aneurysm recurrence compared with coiling in many cases.\r\n* Useful for some complex aneurysms and aneurysms with anatomy unfavorable for endovascular treatment. \r\n\r\nDisadvantages / Complications\r\n\r\n* Requires craniotomy.\r\n* More invasive.\r\n* Longer recovery.\r